00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.370
to solve this kind of
problem, big data problem.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:35.620
If you have terabytes
of data and you

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:40.570
have to scan all the data in
the disc drive in seconds,

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:41.810
how do you do that?

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:44.530
Do you want to [? BGO ?]
on Hadoop cluster?

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:46.990
Do you want to [? BGO ?]
on SPARC cluster,

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:49.150
taking the tens
of minutes or tens

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:55.390
of maybe hours to going through
all the scanning [INAUDIBLE]?

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:58.720
In Google, we don't
have time to wait hours.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.790
Instead, we have to
solve all the problems

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:05.380
like a troubleshooting,
asking queries in seconds.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:08.259
We have to solve all the
big-data-related problems

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:09.280
in seconds.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:14.460
So to do that, we did some
experiment inside Google.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.040
If you want to scan all all
the one terabyte of data

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.720
in one second, how
many disc drives

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:25.140
you would need running in
parallel to do this scanning?

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:29.700
And the result is that you
would need 5,000 to 10,000 disk

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.540
drives running in parallel
to scan all the data

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:36.780
and everything is
done in one second.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.198
And we did that by using the
hundreds of the thousands

00:02:40.198 --> 00:02:42.500
of machines in Google Cloud.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.930
The Google Cloud is the
data center as a computer.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.020
That means it's
not just a building

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:51.810
to host the bunch
of the servers.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:55.560
Instead, it is designed to
work as a massively powerful

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.030
computer, single computer.

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:01.600
So that means we have been
developing all the hardwares

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.070
and middleware stacks
to deploy your own--

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.550
the job or processes,
applications,

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:09.870
on the hundreds of the
servers incidentally

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:12.170
by using a single
line of comments.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:14.880
And the most important part
of the Google Cloud technology

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.560
is the network.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.290
This is on switch
called Jupiter.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.960
This is not a commercial
commodity switch

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.180
we have bought from
other manufacturers.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.300
We have been spending
so much investment

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:32.640
on building our own network
switch hardware from scratch.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:33.930
Why we are doing that?

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.840
Because we are trying to
consolidate all the server

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:40.180
resources like CPUs,
memories, and disc

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:43.560
server storages consolidated
with the microsecond latency

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:49.260
network so that we can
apply this petabit networks

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.260
and infrastructures to
solve many kinds of problems

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:56.012
such as big data solutions or
deep learning technologies.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:02.270
So one of the application
of this petabit network

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:03.950
is a query.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:07.190
That is a massively
parallel processing query.

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:11.150
So that means every single
query you run on BigQuery

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:15.520
will be processed by 1,000
to 2,000 CPU cores, hundreds

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.149
of disc drives in parallel.

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:19.399
So that's the reason
why you're seeing

00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:24.790
the outstanding performance
when you are using BigQuery.

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:27.400
So let's take a look at a
