00:07:22.755 --> 00:07:24.120
of the cloud.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:24.960
Why we can do that?

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:30.550
Because we're using the 1,000
or 2,000 CPU cores and hundreds

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:33.380
of disc drives in parallel
just for a single query.

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:34.574
That is affordable.

00:07:34.574 --> 00:07:35.990
This is the power
of the BigQuery.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:44.540
It's fully managed and
requires no special database

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:45.890
administrator.

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:48.950
And it can process terabyte
of data in tens of seconds--

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:50.060
so fast.

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:53.390
Actually, I can say that, yeah,
I joined Google six years ago

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:56.880
and I essentially noticed
that these technologies,

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:59.270
this technology is
called Dremel in Google,

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:02.552
and we have been using
Dremel since 2006.

00:08:02.552 --> 00:08:04.760
And this is [INAUDIBLE] with
the core technologies we

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:06.500
have been using inside Google.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:08.600
And that is one
reason why Google

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:12.830
has been so successful-- because
every team, like Android teams,

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:16.130
YouTube teams, Chrome
teams, Gmail teams,

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:19.520
can access terabytes of
data in tens of seconds

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:20.900
by using this tool.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:25.100
So that's the largest difference
I've seen when I joined Google

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:27.020
six years ago.

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:29.090
And it also provides
the economy of cloud.

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:32.799
So you don't have to buy
the expensive data warehouse

00:08:32.799 --> 00:08:34.940
appliance which goes, like,
$1 million, $2 million.

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:39.340
But I sometimes
get the feedbacks

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:41.179
from the BigQuery customers.

00:08:41.179 --> 00:08:43.840
I always talk about the
performance of BigQuery,

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:47.890
but they say the performance
is not the highest priority.

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:50.980
For the BigQuery users,
the most important part

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:53.050
is the ease of access.

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:54.100
Why is that?

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:56.440
Because BigQuery is
scalable, because it

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:59.460
shares the 1,000 or
tens of thousands

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:02.740
with CPU cores and disc
storage on Google Cloud.

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:05.470
That means it's so
scalable so even

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:09.080
a single user can learn
a very slow performance

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:14.800
SQL or the SQL that requires
so much server resource.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.530
This doesn't affect to the
overall service or other users

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:22.760
beyond that be affected by
the slow users or slow SQL.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:25.980
So that means you
can allow any users

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:28.600
in your enterprise
accessing to the BigQuery

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:31.570
directory with
their homebrew SQL

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:36.100
or quick and dirty SQL that
is stored on your notepad

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:38.500
or spreadsheet.

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:40.680
Usually if you do these
things with your--

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:44.200
at the existing data warehouse
systems or any database

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:48.540
systems, the database admin
would be upset about that.

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:50.110
So they don't allow it.

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:53.650
But now, BigQuery user
is allowing the people--

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:55.720
the business
people, salespeople,

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:58.000
market researchers,
and support personnel--

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:02.230
to directly execute the SQL
from their spreadsheet or web

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:04.750
UI to BigQuery to
get real-time data.

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:09.310
And still nobody would be
affected for performance.

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:12.940
So we call it the
democratization of data.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:15.490
So now BigQuery
can allow everybody

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:19.680
can access to the enterprise
data in real time.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:22.240
So that's the basic
of the BigQuery.

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:25.880
But still, it's just an
ordinary SQL data warehouse.

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:30.690
So you can write the standard
SQL like the select statement

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:33.990
with where clause and
group by order by.

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:37.100
Those are the same old SQL.

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:39.770
But how you can make it smart?

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:44.030
How we can implement the smarter
analytics by using BigQuery?

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:47.940
Here, I call it a concept
called signatures.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:49.880
By signatures, I mean
the feature vectors

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:54.540
taken from the content so that
you can make the SQL, BigQuery

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:57.250
SQL, a little bit smarter.

00:10:57.250 --> 00:11:01.450
And I can show the demonstration
of this smarter query

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:03.730
by using a demonstration
called QueryIt Smart.

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:07.690
This is a demonstration that we
have built for the next Google

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:08.950
Cloud Next today.

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:12.190
And you can see the
same demonstrations

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:15.190
on the sandbox area,
the playground area.

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:18.310
So anyone who is interested,
you can go to the demo area

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:20.570
and try it by yourself.

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:22.960
If you start the demonstration--

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:27.110
we have the three different
demonstrations inside this demo

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:30.580
and we are choosing
the document search.

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:34.780
And this is where you can
select the key document.

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:37.780
This is all about the
document similarity search.

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:41.650
We have stored over 10
millions of the Stack Overflow

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:44.800
questions stored on BigQuery
as a public data set.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:47.230
And you can do the
similarity search on document

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:48.980
based on its content.

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:51.490
So let me check.

00:11:51.490 --> 00:11:53.920
This one has a key document.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:57.250
So now the query is
executing a query

00:11:57.250 --> 00:12:01.090
on the 10 million documents
from the Stack Overflow

00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:03.010
to find the similar documents.

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:06.160
And this is not about keyword
matching or pattern matching.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:10.450
This is comparing all
the documents each other

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:13.120
to find the similarity
between the documents.

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:17.280
And I got the query finished.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:19.760
So this is the key
document at left.

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:22.000
And in the middle,
you can have the rest

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:23.910
of the similar documents.

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:25.980
So by clicking each
one of them, you

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:31.356
can see the BigQuery was able
to find the similar documents.

00:12:31.356 --> 00:12:32.730
Based on the
[INAUDIBLE], you can

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:35.070
get that-- that
represents the similarity

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:37.470
between the documents.

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:39.590
So how do you implement
this kind of the--

00:12:39.590 --> 00:12:42.370
a little bit smarter
query with BigQuery?

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:44.625
How do you find similar
posts on Stack Overflow?

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:52.160
So to implement this, we have
implemented these things.

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:55.810
At first, you can split
all these statement,

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:58.030
split into the words.

00:12:58.030 --> 00:13:00.580
And then you can
extract the signatures.

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:04.540
By signatures, I mean the
feature vectors for each post.

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:08.860
That represents the
content inside each post.

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:11.330
And you can define the
user-defined function.

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:14.830
User-defined function
is a feature in BigQuery

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:16.600
where you can write
any JavaScript

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:20.260
code to define a
function that could

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:22.690
be used during an SQL query.

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:24.490
So by using
user-defined function,

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:27.700
you can calculate the similarity
between the signatures of two

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:30.840
different documents, and
using user-defined function

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:36.080
in your arbitrary SQL to
find similar documents.

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:40.320
And there is a big difference
between the feature

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:42.980
vector-based search and
the keyword match search.

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:46.890
Traditionally, in any
data warehouse systems,

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:49.700
people have been using
the keyword match vector

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:52.960
specifying the tags for
the content like a movie

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:57.520
or music, actor, and checks if
each document or content has

00:13:57.520 --> 00:13:59.600
certain tags or not.

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:02.350
But instead, what I
[? directly ?] propose

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:05.680
is to use a feature vector
for comparing or searching

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:07.150
for content.

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:09.310
By feature vectors,
you mean that there's

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:12.760
a vector and dimensional
vector that represents

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:16.570
the content of the documents.

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:19.690
So how do you extract
the feature vectors?

00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:21.920
So by taking a look
at this screen,

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:24.240
you can see that the
feature vectors actually

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:28.330
house pairs of the keywords
and scores of keywords.

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:32.710
For example, this
particular document

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:36.320
uses the word add and
grass and element.

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:39.070
This document uses words
such as differences

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:40.900
between or hash map.

00:14:40.900 --> 00:14:43.910
So those are all
important words.

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:47.500
There's some algorithms
used for this extracting

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:49.630
for featured
vectors that can get

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:53.200
rid of all the trivial
words such the in or to

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:55.760
or a or that or there.

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:59.110
So by using the
algorithms called TF-IDF,

00:14:59.110 --> 00:15:01.630
you can extract the
most important word

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:05.170
using the content and put
the scores on each word.

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:07.420
So that is the feature
vector or signature

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:12.190
we can use to do the
similarities between contents.

00:15:12.190 --> 00:15:16.460
So if you put the
these feature vectors

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:21.050
in an n-dimensional space,
you would look like this.

00:15:21.050 --> 00:15:25.400
So if you try to find
the similar documents

00:15:25.400 --> 00:15:28.760
in n-dimensional
space, you have to take

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:30.200
the cosine similarities.

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:32.980
That means if you
have two vectors,

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:37.320
you are looking at the angles
between the two vectors.

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:40.610
So if you had another vector
which has a very narrow angle,

00:15:40.610 --> 00:15:44.390
that means you are finding the
similar documents or content.

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:49.560
And then you can define
a user-defined function

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:51.180
to get the similarity,
calculation

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:53.860
the similarity between the
two different documents.

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:55.140
And it's just a basic math.

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:57.840
It's just a
multiplication, additions

00:15:57.840 --> 00:15:58.980
you learn in high school.

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:00.100
So it's not so hard.

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:01.350
It's not machine learning yet.

