00:36:50.490 --> 00:36:55.020
So you can get a much smarter
search on the BigQuery.

00:36:58.900 --> 00:37:00.360
So how do you do this?

00:37:00.360 --> 00:37:02.830
At first, we have imported
all the 1 million images

00:37:02.830 --> 00:37:05.740
from Wikimedia on the
Google Cloud Storage.

00:37:05.740 --> 00:37:09.370
And then we have used Machine
Learning Engine, ML Engine,

00:37:09.370 --> 00:37:12.600
to learn the convolutional
neural networks on the ML

00:37:12.600 --> 00:37:13.180
Engine.

00:37:13.180 --> 00:37:17.590
This is a typical neural
network design used

00:37:17.590 --> 00:37:20.320
for the analyzing the images.

00:37:20.320 --> 00:37:22.540
But usually, people
are using CNN

00:37:22.540 --> 00:37:25.850
to get the labels for the
images such as, is it a cat?

00:37:25.850 --> 00:37:26.860
Is it a human face?

00:37:26.860 --> 00:37:28.030
Is it a flower?

00:37:28.030 --> 00:37:30.430
But instead of
getting labels, we

00:37:30.430 --> 00:37:32.620
are using CNN to
extract the feature

00:37:32.620 --> 00:37:37.780
vectors that could be in the
signatures of the old images.

00:37:37.780 --> 00:37:40.390
And by using the
BigQuery and UGF,

00:37:40.390 --> 00:37:43.750
you can do this immediately
search against images.

00:37:43.750 --> 00:37:48.190
And we have used VGG16, which
is a popular CNN design.

00:37:48.190 --> 00:37:52.900
And we are extracting the future
vectors from the final fully

00:37:52.900 --> 00:37:55.570
[? connectional ?] layers.

00:37:55.570 --> 00:37:58.590
And you can do this by writing
a very simple TensorFlow

00:37:58.590 --> 00:37:59.710
code like this.

00:37:59.710 --> 00:38:03.270
So you can specify which way you
want to extract a vector from.

00:38:07.540 --> 00:38:09.680
And then it can learn
the GCloud [? command ?]

00:38:09.680 --> 00:38:13.900
to execute a Cloud
ML job to extract

00:38:13.900 --> 00:38:15.520
the features from the images.

00:38:15.520 --> 00:38:19.660
Usually, it takes
hours to learn the jobs

00:38:19.660 --> 00:38:22.410
against the millions of images.

00:38:22.410 --> 00:38:24.190
So then now we
have the signatures

00:38:24.190 --> 00:38:26.110
of images, feature vectors.

00:38:26.110 --> 00:38:30.820
And you can find the similarity
of distance between the vectors

00:38:30.820 --> 00:38:33.670
defined as similar images.

00:38:33.670 --> 00:38:37.630
Again, we are using the UGF
to calculate the similarity

00:38:37.630 --> 00:38:39.670
between the two vectors.

00:38:39.670 --> 00:38:41.820
It's a very simple math.

00:38:41.820 --> 00:38:46.770
And you can use the
UGF in your own SQL,

00:38:46.770 --> 00:38:50.550
rank the ordering by the
distance between the vectors.

00:38:50.550 --> 00:38:53.180
And it's so inexpensive.

00:38:53.180 --> 00:38:56.080
If you run this query
against 1 million images,

00:38:56.080 --> 00:38:58.760
it would only cost $0.86.

00:38:58.760 --> 00:39:02.400
So it's not so expensive.

00:39:02.400 --> 00:39:05.620
